      },
      {
        "type": "richText",
        "role": "section",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Design and Figma Instructions"
          }
        ],
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "type": "image",
        "src": "images/cards-22.png",
        "alt": ""
      },
      {
        "type": "richText",
        "role": "section",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Examples"
          }
        ],
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "type": "image",
        "src": "images/cards-23.png",
        "alt": ""
      },
      {
        "type": "stack",
        "spacing": "normal",
        "children": [
          {
            "type": "richText",
            "role": "section",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Using Design Tokens"
              }
            ],
            "level": 2
          },
          {
            "type": "richText",
            "role": "body",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Design tokens are used across all components in the design system to help standardize values for colors, spacing, text styles, and other design properties. This makes it easier for designers and developers to maintain consistency while building interfaces. Tokens also allow designers to swap values when needed, providing flexibility without requiring components to be detached or rebuilt."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "image",
        "src": "images/cards-24.png",
        "alt": ""
      },
      {
        "type": "image",
        "src": "images/cards-25.png",
        "alt": ""
      },
      {
        "type": "stack",
        "spacing": "normal",
        "children": [
          {
            "type": "richText",
            "role": "section",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Takeaways"
              }
            ],
            "level": 2
          },
          {
            "type": "stack",
            "spacing": "compact",
            "children": [
              {
                "type": "richText",
                "role": "body",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "One key takeaway from designing the card component is that research and feedback are critical steps in the component design process. There are many factors to consider when building a component, including both current and future use cases. Feedback from designers and developers is especially valuable for identifying gaps and improving the component’s usability and flexibility."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "richText",
                "role": "body",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "It is also important to consider the bigger picture. A single component is only one building block within a larger design system, and patterns introduced in one component can influence or be reused across others to create a more interconnected system. Reviewing existing design system components, patterns, and design tokens helps ensure that new components align with established standards and integrate effectively into the broader ecosystem"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
]
